The '''Bornean flat-headed frog''' ('''''Barbourula kalimantanensis''''') is a species of [[frog]] in the family [[Bombinatoridae]].<ref name=frost>{{cite web |url=http://research.amnh.org/vz/herpetology/amphibia/?action=names&taxon=Barbourula+kalimantanensis |title=''Barbourula kalimantanensis'' Iskandar, 1978 |author=Frost, Darrel R. |year=2013 |work=Amphibian Species of the World 5.6, an Online Reference |publisher=American Museum of Natural History |access-date=21 October 2013}}</ref> For many years, it was thought to be the only frog with no [[lung]]s.<ref name="Bickford2008"/> However, [[X-ray_microtomography|micro CT scanning]] revealed that, like all other known frog species, the Bornean flat-headed frog has lungs, though they are tiny.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Blackburn |first1=David C. |last2=Gray |first2=Jaimi A. |last3=Stanley |first3=Edward L. |date=2024-05-20 |title=The only "lungless" frog has a glottis and lungs |journal=Current Biology |language=English |volume=34 |issue=10 |pages=R492–R493 |doi=10.1016/j.cub.2024.03.017 |issn=0960-9822 |pmid=38772334|bibcode=2024CBio...34.R492B |doi-access=free }}</ref>

[[Djoko Iskandar]], an Indonesian zoologist, first described the Bornean flat-headed frog based on a single specimen.<ref name="Iskandar1978">{{cite journal | last = Iskandar | first = Djoko T. | author-link = Djoko Iskandar | year = 1978 | title = A new species of ''Barbourula'': first record of a discoglossid anuran in Borneo | journal = [[Copeia]] | volume = 1978 | issue = 4 | pages = 564&ndash;566 | doi = 10.2307/1443681 | jstor = 1443681 }}</ref> However, he did not think the frog was lungless until he was in the field with David Bickford's expedition. Bickford ''et al.'' dissected some of the specimens and were unable to see the lungs, leading them to incorrectly conclude that no lungs were present.<ref name="Bickford2008">{{cite journal | last = Bickford | first = David |author2=Iskandar, Djoko |author3=Barlian, Anggraini | year = 2008 | title = A lungless frog discovered on Borneo | journal = Current Biology | volume = 18 | issue = 9 | pages = R374&ndash;R375 | doi = 10.1016/j.cub.2008.03.010 | pmid = 18424139 | s2cid = 1427952 | doi-access = free | bibcode = 2008CBio...18.R374B }}{{Retracted|doi=10.1016/j.cub.2024.07.093|pmid=39096905|intentional=yes}}</ref>

==Description==
This frog grows to a snout-to-vent length of {{convert|77|mm|in|abbr=on}} for females and males are slightly smaller. It has a dorsoventrally flattened body with a broad head with a rounded snout. The limbs are robust and both hands and feet are fully webbed. The dorsal skin is smooth with small [[tubercle]]s on the posterior end and the hind legs and the ventral skin is smooth. Its general colour is brown with black mottling.<ref name=AmphibiaWeb>{{cite web |url=http://amphibiaweb.org/cgi-bin/amphib_query?where-genus=Barbourula&where-species=kalimantanensis |title=''Barbourula kalimantanensis'' |author=Boisvert, Adam  |date=2008-11-24 |work=AmphibiaWeb |access-date=2013-12-11}}</ref>

==Distribution and habitat==
This aquatic frog lives in cold, clear, and fast rivers in remote areas of the rain forests of [[Kalimantan]], the [[Indonesia]]n part of the island of [[Borneo]]. It is known from only two localities, both in the [[Kapuas River]] basin.<ref name="Bickford2008"/>

==Status==
The Bornean flat-headed frog is listed as [[Endangered]] by the [[IUCN]]. It is threatened by [[habitat loss]] from severe degradation of the river habitats because of increased turbidity and toxic metals used in mining and other negative consequences of development on the island.<ref name="iucn status 17 November 2021" /><ref name="Bickford2008"/>

